1
00:00:27,235 --> 00:00:29,320
Good evening. Our lead story.

2
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:34,159
ENCOM CEO and video game icon 
Kevin Flynn has disappeared.

3
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:37,287
He was best known for designing Tron 
and Space Paranoids,

4
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:40,123
the two bestselling video games 
in history.

5
00:00:40,248 --> 00:00:43,376
Flynn took ownership of ENCOM 
in 1982

6
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:46,838
as the company skyrocketed 
to the top of the tech industry.

7
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:51,217
But things changed in 1985 
with the untimely death of Flynn's wife,

8
00:00:51,342 --> 00:00:53,845
the mother of his young son, Sam.

9
00:00:53,928 --> 00:00:55,930
Recently, ENCOM board members

10
00:00:56,014 --> 00:00:57,223
have been troubled by reports

11
00:00:57,348 --> 00:01:00,310
of Flynn's erratic, 
even obsessive, behavior.

12
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:04,105
With Flynn missing, 
the company is now in chaos.

13
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:06,399
This afternoon, ENCOM's board

14
00:01:06,524 --> 00:01:09,694
moved to seize control 
from Flynn's partner, Alan Bradley,

15
00:01:09,778 --> 00:01:12,781
vowing to return the company 
to profitability.

16
00:01:13,156 --> 00:01:18,077
Loyal to the end, Bradley maintains 
his belief that Flynn is not missing

17
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:20,747
and is instead pursuing his dream of

18
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:24,292
"a digital frontier 
to reshape the human condition."

19
00:03:04,684 --> 00:03:06,394
Now that is a big door.

20
00:03:06,477 --> 00:03:07,770
Welcome, everybody.

21
00:03:08,146 --> 00:03:11,399
Please settle. I know it's late, 
so I'll skip the pleasantries

22
00:03:11,482 --> 00:03:15,195
and hand things over to our chairman, 
Richard Mackey.

23
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:20,116
Thank you. Thank you. 
Thank you, Claire.

24
00:03:22,076 --> 00:03:23,786
Tonight I am pleased to announce

25
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:29,834
that ENCOM's last fiscal year 
was our most profitable ever.

26
00:03:30,752 --> 00:03:31,794
Yes.

27
00:03:50,521 --> 00:03:53,942
At midnight the 1 2th version 
of our flagship operating system

28
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:56,402
will hit the shelves around the world.

29
00:03:57,862 --> 00:04:00,281
I'm sure it comes as no surprise 
whom we have to thank.

30
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:02,450
The head of our software design team,

31
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:07,497
a man whose father's 
own notable history with ENCOM

32
00:04:07,622 --> 00:04:10,333
helped make this company 
what it is today.

33
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:15,380
Edward Dillinger. Well done.

34
00:04:15,797 --> 00:04:17,257
Well done.

35
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:25,223
And this year, simultaneous 
to the release of ENCOM OS-12,

36
00:04:25,974 --> 00:04:31,312
we will be making our debut 
on Tokyo's Nikkei Index.

37
00:04:31,437 --> 00:04:36,317
ENCOM's stock will now trade 
around the world 2 4l7.

38
00:04:36,401 --> 00:04:38,945
Huh? Oh, yes.

39
00:05:02,844 --> 00:05:05,555
- Excuse me. 
- Yes, Alan?

40
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:07,140
Given the prices

41
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:10,893
that we charge to students and schools,

42
00:05:11,894 --> 00:05:14,564
what sort of improvements 
have been made in Flynn...

43
00:05:15,106 --> 00:05:18,318
I mean, ENCOM OS-12?

44
00:05:21,487 --> 00:05:24,866
This year we put a "12" on the box.

45
00:05:25,825 --> 00:05:29,245
OS-12 is the most secure 
operating system ever released.

46
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:34,000
The idea of sharing our software 
or giving it away for free

47
00:05:34,584 --> 00:05:36,919
disappeared with Kevin Flynn.

48
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:52,602
I know you're here.

49
00:05:53,186 --> 00:05:55,104
Let's make this easy.

50
00:05:57,982 --> 00:05:59,108
Come on, come on.

51
00:06:07,575 --> 00:06:08,826
Freeze!

52
00:06:09,619 --> 00:06:10,661
Okay.

53
00:06:11,037 --> 00:06:12,497
If there are no more questions,

54
00:06:13,081 --> 00:06:16,125
I would like you to meet 
our next blockbuster.

55
00:06:16,209 --> 00:06:18,961
ENCOM OS-12.

56
00:06:20,838 --> 00:06:22,256
Whoa.

57
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,634
Still a few bugs.

58
00:06:24,759 --> 00:06:26,385
What's that? What is that?

59
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:32,475
Just bear with us one sec.

60
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,727
Make this go away now.

61
00:06:34,936 --> 00:06:38,981
...where ENCOM's chairman, 
Richard Mackey, will be launching...

62
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:42,443
- We go live in 30 seconds. 
- He's on the roof!

63
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:45,321
- Anyone know where the master file is? 
- I do.

64
00:06:46,823 --> 00:06:48,074
It's on the Web.

65
00:06:50,326 --> 00:06:51,994
We've got to stop this.

66
00:06:56,082 --> 00:06:58,334
I've got to ring that opening bell.

67
00:06:58,835 --> 00:07:01,045
- Make this stop now. 
- It's locked.

68
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:04,048
Just shut it down. Shut it down! Now!

69
00:07:04,173 --> 00:07:07,135
Relax, Mackey. Relax.

70
00:07:07,426 --> 00:07:08,553
It's under control.

71
00:07:08,678 --> 00:07:10,680
Relax? Our most valuable

72
00:07:10,805 --> 00:07:14,308
and most secure operating system 
is out there for free!

73
00:07:14,433 --> 00:07:16,102
How am I supposed to explain that?

74
00:07:16,185 --> 00:07:19,147
I don't know. 
Say it was all part of the plan.

75
00:07:20,189 --> 00:07:22,316
A little gift from ENCOM.

76
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:26,487
Apparently, ENCOM Technology 
is having some technical difficulties.

77
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:30,992
Why don't we give them a chance 
to sort things out?

78
00:07:58,102 --> 00:08:00,062
Hey, Dad.

79
00:08:00,229 --> 00:08:01,981
How you doing?

80
00:08:08,029 --> 00:08:11,657
- Where you gonna run to now? 
- Hey, you don't wanna do that.

81
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:14,035
Didn't anyone tell you? 
Stealing is wrong.

82
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,746
You can't steal 
what was designed to be free.

83
00:08:16,871 --> 00:08:19,999
- Now I got you. 
- Your boss is okay with this.

84
00:08:20,082 --> 00:08:21,375
The hell he is.

85
00:08:21,834 --> 00:08:23,586
Your boss works for the CEO

86
00:08:23,711 --> 00:08:25,296
and the CEO 
works for the shareholders.

87
00:08:25,421 --> 00:08:27,715
Do you know 
who the biggest shareholder is?

88
00:08:28,716 --> 00:08:30,301
I don't know. Some kid.

89
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:36,224
You're Mr. Flynn?

90
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:39,602
Why?

91
00:08:40,269 --> 00:08:42,605
This is your father's company.

92
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:46,234
- Not anymore. 
- What? Hey. Hey, kid.

93
00:08:46,359 --> 00:08:48,986
No!

94
00:09:00,957 --> 00:09:02,124
Yeah.

95
00:09:02,625 --> 00:09:04,919
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

96
00:09:09,465 --> 00:09:10,675
You kidding me?

97
00:09:11,676 --> 00:09:15,388
- That's him! Get the team down here! 
- There he is! Get him!

98
00:09:17,056 --> 00:09:18,558
What the...

99
00:09:18,849 --> 00:09:20,977
Hey! No free ride!

100
00:09:21,644 --> 00:09:24,146
- Whoa! 
- No free taxi!

101
00:09:24,397 --> 00:09:26,148
You pay!

102
00:09:28,818 --> 00:09:31,362
- Whoa, whoa, whoa! 
- No free ride!

103
00:09:40,997 --> 00:09:44,000
Hold it right there. Freeze!

104
00:09:45,918 --> 00:09:49,297
Okay, boys. You got me.

105
00:09:50,131 --> 00:09:51,215
Hey.

106
00:10:56,864 --> 00:10:58,949
- Hey, Karl. 
- Hey, how you doing?

107
00:11:14,924 --> 00:11:17,301
Enjoy it, Marv. You earned it.

108
00:11:21,347 --> 00:11:23,140
Why are you in my apartment, Alan?

109
00:11:25,059 --> 00:11:27,144
You don't answer your phone.

110
00:11:29,647 --> 00:11:31,565
- How you been, Sam? 
- Oh, you know,

111
00:11:31,690 --> 00:11:35,319
when I was 12, I appreciated 
the surrogate-father thing. But come on.

112
00:11:36,112 --> 00:11:37,988
I got it all under control now.

113
00:11:38,406 --> 00:11:40,491
Oh. Clearly!

114
00:11:40,574 --> 00:11:41,826
You want to help me 
with my homework?

115
00:11:42,034 --> 00:11:43,369
Like old times? Have a catch?

116
00:11:45,788 --> 00:11:49,250
You know, 
you got a pretty nice view here.

117
00:11:51,836 --> 00:11:55,214
Heard you did a triple axel off of her 
a few hours ago.

118
00:11:56,298 --> 00:11:59,093
- Rough landing, huh? 
- Could have been worse.

119
00:11:59,218 --> 00:12:02,012
I also thought your message 
to the board

120
00:12:02,096 --> 00:12:03,222
was very clever.

121
00:12:03,347 --> 00:12:05,808
Did you like that? That was Marv's idea.

122
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:14,233
Alan, are we really 
going to do this again?

123
00:12:15,109 --> 00:12:18,195
Do I really look like I'm ready 
to run a Fortune 500 company?

124
00:12:18,738 --> 00:12:19,947
No.

125
00:12:20,239 --> 00:12:24,285
And truthfully, the company 
is pretty happy with where you are, too.

126
00:12:24,702 --> 00:12:25,661
I bet.

127
00:12:25,828 --> 00:12:28,956
That way they can keep doing 
whatever they want.

128
00:12:30,666 --> 00:12:35,087
You know, I guess what I find curious 
is the crazy charities,

129
00:12:35,337 --> 00:12:37,631
the annual prank on the company.

130
00:12:38,048 --> 00:12:40,593
You have an interesting way 
of being disinterested, Sam.

131
00:12:40,718 --> 00:12:42,511
Why are you here, Alan?

132
00:12:45,890 --> 00:12:47,558
I was paged last night.

133
00:12:47,725 --> 00:12:49,894
Oh, man, still rocking the pager?

134
00:12:50,102 --> 00:12:51,604
Good for you.

135
00:12:51,687 --> 00:12:56,108
Yeah, your dad once told me 
I had to sleep with it, and I still do.

136
00:12:57,276 --> 00:12:59,570
Page came from your dad's office 
at the arcade.

137
00:13:03,365 --> 00:13:05,743
- So? 
- So?

138
00:13:06,952 --> 00:13:09,538
That number's been disconnected 
for 20 years.

139
00:13:10,956 --> 00:13:14,502
Sam, two nights before he disappeared, 
he came to my house.

140
00:13:14,710 --> 00:13:17,254
"I've cracked it," he kept saying.

141
00:13:17,379 --> 00:13:21,133
He was talking about genetic 
algorithms, quantum teleportation.

142
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:25,513
He said he was about to change 
everything. Science, medicine, religion.

143
00:13:26,847 --> 00:13:29,099
He wouldn't have left that, Sam.

144
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:33,521
He wouldn't have left you.

145
00:13:33,729 --> 00:13:37,274
Oh, Alan. You're the only one 
who still believes that.

146
00:13:37,608 --> 00:13:40,444
He's either dead 
or chilling in Costa Rica.

147
00:13:41,487 --> 00:13:43,072
Probably both.

148
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:45,282
Look, I'm sorry.

149
00:13:45,366 --> 00:13:47,868
I'm tired, and I smell like jail.

150
00:13:48,619 --> 00:13:52,915
Let's just reconvene in another couple 
of years, huh? What do you say?

151
00:13:56,961 --> 00:13:58,128
Here.

152
00:13:59,338 --> 00:14:01,715
These are the keys to the arcade.

153
00:14:02,591 --> 00:14:07,596
I haven't gone over there yet. 
I thought you should be the one.

154
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:11,058
Alan, you're acting like I'm gonna 
find him sitting there working.

155
00:14:11,225 --> 00:14:14,979
Just, "Hey, kiddo. Lost track of time."

156
00:14:18,566 --> 00:14:20,651
Wouldn't that be something?

157
00:14:28,909 --> 00:14:31,453
What?

158
00:17:47,399 --> 00:17:49,068
You son of a gun.

159
00:18:29,942 --> 00:18:31,443
Flynn.

160
00:18:37,449 --> 00:18:38,659
Huh.

161
00:18:39,868 --> 00:18:41,787
Let's try the back door.

162
00:18:45,874 --> 00:18:47,251
What were you working on?

163
00:18:54,132 --> 00:18:55,759
LaserControl.

164
00:18:56,593 --> 00:18:57,970
Okay.

165
00:19:38,552 --> 00:19:40,637
This isn't happening.

166
00:19:56,445 --> 00:19:58,322
Oh, man, this is happening.

167
00:20:07,497 --> 00:20:08,832
Wait.

168
00:20:10,292 --> 00:20:12,502
This program has no disk. 
Another stray.

169
00:20:12,628 --> 00:20:13,629
Hey, wait.

170
00:20:13,879 --> 00:20:16,465
Wait. I'm not a program!

171
00:20:45,577 --> 00:20:47,371
He actually did it.

172
00:20:50,999 --> 00:20:52,542
I'm on the Grid.

173
00:21:02,177 --> 00:21:05,514
Does the name Kevin Flynn 
mean anything to you?

174
00:21:05,597 --> 00:21:07,516
Be quiet if you want to live!

175
00:21:07,599 --> 00:21:09,851
Not the games, not the games,

176
00:21:09,935 --> 00:21:12,354
not the games, not the games...

177
00:21:12,437 --> 00:21:13,772
What's his problem?

178
00:21:16,775 --> 00:21:18,568
Not the games, not the...

179
00:22:05,824 --> 00:22:07,534
Rectify.

180
00:22:10,871 --> 00:22:12,456
Rectify.

181
00:22:15,250 --> 00:22:18,879
- Games. 
- No. Please!

182
00:22:19,004 --> 00:22:20,047
Rectify.

183
00:22:20,172 --> 00:22:23,175
You probably get this a lot, 
but there's been a big mistake.

184
00:22:23,258 --> 00:22:24,968
Games.

185
00:22:24,968 --> 00:22:26,762
- Please, no, no. 
- Whoa. Hey.

186
00:23:08,053 --> 00:23:11,056
Er, can somebody tell me what the...

187
00:23:15,685 --> 00:23:17,896
Hey, hey. It's got a zipper.

188
00:23:35,539 --> 00:23:36,998
He is different.

189
00:23:45,882 --> 00:23:46,967
This can't be good.

190
00:23:56,810 --> 00:24:01,731
Attention, program. 
You will receive an identity disk.

191
00:24:01,940 --> 00:24:07,446
Everything you do or learn 
will be imprinted on this disk.

192
00:24:08,488 --> 00:24:12,409
If you lose your disk 
or fail to follow commands,

193
00:24:12,534 --> 00:24:16,329
you will be subject 
to immediate de-resolution.

194
00:24:22,544 --> 00:24:25,797
Mirroring complete. 
Disk activated and synchronized.

195
00:24:26,047 --> 00:24:27,340
Proceed to games.

196
00:24:28,925 --> 00:24:30,802
Games?

197
00:24:34,681 --> 00:24:36,308
Where are you going?

198
00:24:43,273 --> 00:24:44,649
What am I supposed to do?

199
00:24:45,692 --> 00:24:47,485
Survive.

200
00:25:39,621 --> 00:25:43,291
All combatants, 
prepare for disk wars.

201
00:25:43,416 --> 00:25:46,878
Disk wars! Disk wars!

202
00:25:47,837 --> 00:25:49,130
Disk wars!

203
00:25:50,257 --> 00:25:51,758
Disk wars!

204
00:25:53,051 --> 00:25:55,011
Disk wars!

205
00:26:21,037 --> 00:26:23,373
Platform 8.

206
00:26:23,456 --> 00:26:25,667
I have a three-inch version of you 
on my shelf.

207
00:26:25,792 --> 00:26:28,586
Combatants 3 and 11.

208
00:26:28,753 --> 00:26:30,463
Disk wars.

209
00:26:40,599 --> 00:26:42,851
You definitely didn't do that.

210
00:27:14,507 --> 00:27:15,592
Damn it!

211
00:27:24,517 --> 00:27:25,977
So that's how it is.

212
00:27:42,744 --> 00:27:46,081
Combatant 11, de-resolution.

213
00:27:46,998 --> 00:27:49,918
I won. Now let me out!

214
00:27:50,043 --> 00:27:53,880
Combatant 3, round one, victory.

215
00:27:54,672 --> 00:27:56,716
No unusual activity on the Grid.

216
00:27:56,841 --> 00:27:58,301
Security sweeps and patrols

217
00:27:58,468 --> 00:28:00,970
have been intensified. 
Rectifier on schedule.

218
00:28:01,054 --> 00:28:04,933
Your initiative should be 
fully operational within 12 cycles.

219
00:28:06,601 --> 00:28:09,104
Perhaps if you were to include me

220
00:28:09,229 --> 00:28:13,817
in this initiative, 
I could be of even greater service.

221
00:28:13,900 --> 00:28:15,026
Combatants 3 and 7, disk wars.

222
00:28:15,026 --> 00:28:18,154
Combatants 3 and 7, disk wars.

223
00:28:20,907 --> 00:28:22,534
Initiate.

224
00:28:25,704 --> 00:28:29,082
Yeah. I'm out.

225
00:28:35,922 --> 00:28:38,049
Combatant 3, victory.

226
00:28:43,096 --> 00:28:45,265
Combatant 3, violation.

227
00:28:46,433 --> 00:28:50,061
Combatant 6, de-resolution.

228
00:28:50,145 --> 00:28:52,188
What is that program?

229
00:28:53,273 --> 00:28:54,733
Combatant 3,

230
00:28:54,858 --> 00:28:56,818
violation.

231
00:29:02,115 --> 00:29:04,200
Initiate final round.

232
00:29:08,705 --> 00:29:11,750
Combatant 3 versus Rinzler.

233
00:29:11,833 --> 00:29:14,377
Rinzler!

234
00:29:14,461 --> 00:29:16,004
Rinzler!

235
00:29:19,048 --> 00:29:20,759
You gotta be kidding me.

236
00:29:21,342 --> 00:29:22,385
Rinzler!

237
00:29:23,970 --> 00:29:25,722
Rinzler!

238
00:29:26,473 --> 00:29:27,807
Rinzler!

239
00:29:29,184 --> 00:29:30,268
Rinzler!

240
00:29:31,352 --> 00:29:32,645
Rinzler!

241
00:29:40,653 --> 00:29:42,655
Come on. Is that even legal?

242
00:30:16,189 --> 00:30:19,067
Why do I feel like I just got dumped on?

243
00:30:25,073 --> 00:30:27,742
Come on. Huh? Come on.

244
00:31:10,326 --> 00:31:11,953
De-rez!

245
00:31:13,371 --> 00:31:14,956
De-rez!

246
00:31:15,915 --> 00:31:17,208
De-rez!

247
00:31:21,588 --> 00:31:23,673
User.

248
00:31:34,767 --> 00:31:37,478
Identify yourself, program.

249
00:31:38,771 --> 00:31:41,024
I'm not a program.

250
00:31:46,571 --> 00:31:48,114
Identify.

251
00:31:49,782 --> 00:31:52,201
My name is Sam Flynn.

252
00:31:56,539 --> 00:31:58,708
Bring him to me.

253
00:32:01,294 --> 00:32:02,337
Let me go.

254
00:32:06,299 --> 00:32:07,634
Freaks.

255
00:32:09,844 --> 00:32:10,845
Where am I?

256
00:32:13,765 --> 00:32:16,267
Am I on the Grid?

257
00:32:17,644 --> 00:32:19,228
Who are you?

258
00:32:24,817 --> 00:32:25,902
Dad.

259
00:32:26,486 --> 00:32:28,237
Sam.

260
00:32:28,321 --> 00:32:31,449
Look at you, man. 
Look at the size of you.

261
00:32:33,493 --> 00:32:37,121
- How did you get in here? 
- I got your message.

262
00:32:37,246 --> 00:32:40,166
Oh. So it's just you?

263
00:32:41,167 --> 00:32:42,126
Yeah.

264
00:32:42,251 --> 00:32:46,089
Just you. Oh.

265
00:32:46,172 --> 00:32:48,925
Isn't this something?

266
00:32:49,008 --> 00:32:50,259
You look the same.

267
00:32:50,343 --> 00:32:53,471
A lot's happened, Sam. 
More than you can imagine. Disk.

268
00:32:58,810 --> 00:33:00,436
Let's have a look.

269
00:33:16,369 --> 00:33:17,870
Got it.

270
00:33:18,371 --> 00:33:20,498
Hmm. I expected more.

271
00:33:24,210 --> 00:33:26,921
So, you were trapped in here.

272
00:33:27,046 --> 00:33:28,506
That's right.

273
00:33:31,718 --> 00:33:34,762
- And you're in charge. 
- Right again. You're two for two.

274
00:33:35,596 --> 00:33:38,141
So can we just go home now?

275
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:41,436
Not in the cards.

276
00:33:42,562 --> 00:33:43,896
Not for you.

277
00:33:48,067 --> 00:33:50,361
That's a hell of a way to treat your son.

278
00:33:52,321 --> 00:33:54,198
Oh, that.

279
00:33:58,870 --> 00:34:01,330
I'm not your father, Sam.

280
00:34:03,249 --> 00:34:06,502
But I'm very, very happy to see you.

281
00:34:09,797 --> 00:34:11,716
Clu.

282
00:34:14,469 --> 00:34:15,678
Where is he?

283
00:34:15,803 --> 00:34:17,889
What did you do to him?

284
00:34:18,014 --> 00:34:22,560
Same thing I'm going to do to you, user.

285
00:34:44,665 --> 00:34:47,335
Greetings, programs.

286
00:34:50,046 --> 00:34:53,633
Oh, what an occasion 
we have here before us.

287
00:34:54,258 --> 00:34:58,096
Because your rumors are true.

288
00:34:58,221 --> 00:35:03,017
We do indeed have in our midst

289
00:35:03,142 --> 00:35:05,228
a user!

290
00:35:07,438 --> 00:35:10,107
A user.

291
00:35:12,485 --> 00:35:14,737
So, what to do?

292
00:35:15,530 --> 00:35:20,118
What does this user deserve?

293
00:35:20,201 --> 00:35:22,537
Might I suggest perhaps 
the challenge of the Grid?

294
00:35:29,710 --> 00:35:34,674
And who best to battle 
this singular opponent?

295
00:35:36,008 --> 00:35:40,138
Perhaps one who has some 
experience in these matters.

296
00:35:43,474 --> 00:35:45,268
Oh, yes, indeed, programs.

297
00:35:46,185 --> 00:35:48,646
Your liberator!

298
00:35:48,771 --> 00:35:50,356
Your luminary!

299
00:35:51,858 --> 00:35:53,609
Your leader and maker!

300
00:35:55,361 --> 00:35:58,906
The one who vanquished 
the tyranny of the user

301
00:35:59,657 --> 00:36:02,326
those many cycles before!

302
00:36:03,369 --> 00:36:05,913
I've been waiting 
a long time for this.

303
00:36:06,038 --> 00:36:09,584
Clu!

304
00:36:09,709 --> 00:36:12,837
You wanna play? I'll play.

305
00:36:30,354 --> 00:36:32,231
What's this?

306
00:36:32,315 --> 00:36:34,108
What do I do with this?

307
00:36:34,233 --> 00:36:36,277
I'll give you a hint.

308
00:36:37,153 --> 00:36:38,571
Not that.

309
00:36:48,456 --> 00:36:49,916
Grid is live.

310
00:36:50,041 --> 00:36:53,502
Initiate light cycle battle.

311
00:37:08,392 --> 00:37:09,727
You got no chance, user.

312
00:37:09,852 --> 00:37:13,022
Their bikes are faster than ours. 
Use the levels.

313
00:37:18,361 --> 00:37:20,446
Now this I can do.

314
00:37:52,853 --> 00:37:54,272
Here we go.

315
00:39:00,296 --> 00:39:01,714
Come on. Come on!

316
00:39:18,064 --> 00:39:21,025
Hey! We gotta work together.

317
00:39:21,150 --> 00:39:23,110
It's the only way.

318
00:39:31,202 --> 00:39:33,746
That's it. You got me.

319
00:39:41,670 --> 00:39:43,047
Boo!

320
00:39:46,133 --> 00:39:47,426
- Whoo-hoo! 
- Yeah!

321
00:39:47,510 --> 00:39:49,637
Now that's what I'm talking about.

322
00:39:49,970 --> 00:39:52,431
Another customer. Let's go!

323
00:40:10,032 --> 00:40:11,450
Hang on, buddy, I'm coming.

324
00:40:20,584 --> 00:40:21,710
Come on.

325
00:40:35,391 --> 00:40:37,852
This is it. Come on.

326
00:41:07,423 --> 00:41:09,341
Illegal combatant on the Grid.

327
00:41:09,425 --> 00:41:10,843
Get in.

328
00:41:10,926 --> 00:41:12,470
Illegal combatant on the Grid.

329
00:41:12,595 --> 00:41:13,721
Get in!

330
00:41:19,935 --> 00:41:23,147
System failure. Release Rinzler.

331
00:41:23,272 --> 00:41:25,232
Game on, old friend.

332
00:41:34,491 --> 00:41:37,745
- Who are you? 
- Hang on.

333
00:42:05,731 --> 00:42:07,274
Pull up, man, you can't make that!

334
00:42:16,867 --> 00:42:19,245
Made it.

335
00:42:28,337 --> 00:42:29,505
I'm Quorra.

336
00:42:35,010 --> 00:42:36,220
They're turning around.

337
00:42:36,345 --> 00:42:37,680
Not by choice.

338
00:42:37,805 --> 00:42:41,350
Their vehicles can't go off Grid. 
They'll malfunction on this terrain.

339
00:42:41,475 --> 00:42:44,812
- What about us? 
- Obviously not.

340
00:42:59,285 --> 00:43:00,869
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

341
00:44:00,471 --> 00:44:02,514
Wait here.

342
00:44:05,893 --> 00:44:07,311
Quorra.

343
00:44:08,395 --> 00:44:10,356
I dreamed of Tron.

344
00:44:11,482 --> 00:44:13,484
First time in years.

345
00:44:13,984 --> 00:44:15,569
It's a sign.

346
00:44:16,904 --> 00:44:20,240
A sign, my dear apprentice, 
of a weary soul.

347
00:44:21,408 --> 00:44:24,620
- I'm afraid something's happened. 
- Something has happened.

348
00:44:25,954 --> 00:44:27,539
We have a guest.

349
00:44:28,999 --> 00:44:31,502
There are no guests, kiddo.

350
00:44:55,109 --> 00:44:57,152
Sam...

351
00:44:58,237 --> 00:45:00,572
Long time.

352
00:45:02,074 --> 00:45:04,910
You have no idea.

353
00:45:10,666 --> 00:45:13,252
You're... You're here.

354
00:45:17,339 --> 00:45:18,799
You're here.

355
00:45:19,675 --> 00:45:22,094
I'm here.

356
00:45:29,685 --> 00:45:31,228
You're big.

357
00:45:32,646 --> 00:45:34,440
You're...

358
00:45:35,190 --> 00:45:36,525
Old.

359
00:45:40,237 --> 00:45:42,489
How did you get here?

360
00:45:42,614 --> 00:45:45,075
Alan came over.

361
00:45:45,826 --> 00:45:47,536
Bradley.

362
00:45:47,661 --> 00:45:50,456
Yeah. He got your page.

363
00:45:50,539 --> 00:45:53,792
I found your office under the arcade.

364
00:45:55,210 --> 00:45:56,670
Page...

365
00:45:57,838 --> 00:45:59,089
Oh, the page.

366
00:46:00,424 --> 00:46:02,009
Of course.

367
00:46:06,096 --> 00:46:08,182
Clu had him on the light cycle grid.

368
00:46:08,307 --> 00:46:10,434
I intervened.

369
00:46:11,101 --> 00:46:12,144
Oh.

370
00:46:14,271 --> 00:46:15,272
Dinner soon.

371
00:46:16,231 --> 00:46:18,650
We'll talk then.

372
00:46:32,623 --> 00:46:35,834
He never thought he'd see you again.

373
00:46:38,045 --> 00:46:39,713
Yeah.

374
00:46:46,887 --> 00:46:51,225
Vintage. Flynn built it 
many cycles ago for the games.

375
00:46:52,309 --> 00:46:55,187
It doesn't get out 
as much as it used to,

376
00:46:55,270 --> 00:46:58,232
but it's still the fastest thing on the Grid.

377
00:47:28,720 --> 00:47:31,098
Your move, Flynn, come on.

378
00:47:32,015 --> 00:47:34,518
Come on!

379
00:47:56,206 --> 00:48:00,502
- How old are you now, Sam? 
- You should be 27.

380
00:48:01,587 --> 00:48:03,547
Yes, 27.

381
00:48:03,672 --> 00:48:07,050
- Do you attend a college? 
- Caltech.

382
00:48:08,427 --> 00:48:12,180
Caltech. My alma mater.

383
00:48:12,306 --> 00:48:13,599
Yeah.

384
00:48:13,682 --> 00:48:15,309
Till I dropped out.

385
00:48:24,776 --> 00:48:25,944
Work?

386
00:48:26,612 --> 00:48:29,406
- Job? ENCOM? Are you... 
- No.

387
00:48:31,700 --> 00:48:34,077
I check in once a year.

388
00:48:35,495 --> 00:48:37,623
- Wife, girlfriend? 
- Dog.

389
00:48:38,123 --> 00:48:40,459
Marvin.

390
00:48:40,542 --> 00:48:42,669
- A rescue. 
- Well, dogs...

391
00:48:42,753 --> 00:48:44,504
Dogs are cool.

392
00:48:53,555 --> 00:48:57,476
I'm sure you must have 
a few questions of your own, Sam.

393
00:48:59,811 --> 00:49:01,855
Actually, just one.

394
00:49:05,233 --> 00:49:07,194
Why I never came home.

395
00:49:11,073 --> 00:49:13,825
Those nights when I went to the office,

396
00:49:13,909 --> 00:49:17,204
I'm sure you've figured it out by now, 
I was coming here.

397
00:49:18,372 --> 00:49:21,166
Human form into digital space.

398
00:49:21,249 --> 00:49:23,293
Heavy stuff.

399
00:49:25,295 --> 00:49:27,506
But I also had you.

400
00:49:27,589 --> 00:49:31,051
I had ENCOM. 
I couldn't be in here all the time.

401
00:49:31,134 --> 00:49:33,845
I needed partners to help out.

402
00:49:33,929 --> 00:49:35,597
Tron and Clu?

403
00:49:36,431 --> 00:49:37,557
That's right.

404
00:49:40,268 --> 00:49:43,730
Tron was created by Alan 
for the old system.

405
00:49:43,814 --> 00:49:45,941
I brought him here to protect this one.

406
00:49:46,066 --> 00:49:48,276
Clu was my creation.

407
00:49:48,402 --> 00:49:52,197
A program designed 
to create a perfect world.

408
00:49:52,280 --> 00:49:56,076
We were jamming, man, 
building utopia.

409
00:49:56,201 --> 00:49:58,704
Hours in here 
were just minutes back home.

410
00:49:58,787 --> 00:50:01,581
Just when I thought 
it couldn't get any more profound,

411
00:50:01,665 --> 00:50:03,834
something unexpected happened.

412
00:50:04,501 --> 00:50:05,752
The miracle.

413
00:50:06,503 --> 00:50:08,422
The miracle.

414
00:50:08,505 --> 00:50:10,132
You remember.

415
00:50:11,466 --> 00:50:14,636
ISOs. Isomorphic algorithms.

416
00:50:15,470 --> 00:50:19,182
- A whole new life form. 
- And you created them?

417
00:50:23,812 --> 00:50:25,272
No. No.

418
00:50:25,355 --> 00:50:28,942
They manifested, like a flame.

419
00:50:29,026 --> 00:50:31,945
They weren't really from anywhere.

420
00:50:32,029 --> 00:50:34,906
The conditions were right 
and they came into being.

421
00:50:35,991 --> 00:50:39,786
For centuries we've dreamed 
of gods, spirits, aliens,

422
00:50:39,953 --> 00:50:41,955
an intelligence beyond our own.

423
00:50:42,038 --> 00:50:43,331
You seeing this?

424
00:50:43,457 --> 00:50:48,086
I found them in here. 
Like flowers in a wasteland.

425
00:50:51,006 --> 00:50:53,550
Profoundly naive.

426
00:50:55,302 --> 00:50:58,013
Unimaginably wise.

427
00:50:59,723 --> 00:51:01,933
They were spectacular.

428
00:51:02,017 --> 00:51:04,811
Everything I'd hoped to find 
in the system,

429
00:51:04,936 --> 00:51:07,439
control, order, perfection,

430
00:51:07,522 --> 00:51:08,899
none of it meant a thing.

431
00:51:09,024 --> 00:51:10,275
I'd been living in a hall of mirrors.

432
00:51:11,318 --> 00:51:13,987
The ISOs shattered it.

433
00:51:14,071 --> 00:51:17,657
The possibilities of their root code, 
their digital DNA.

434
00:51:17,741 --> 00:51:20,160
Disease? History.

435
00:51:20,952 --> 00:51:22,579
Science, philosophy,

436
00:51:22,704 --> 00:51:26,875
every idea man has ever had 
about the universe up for grabs.

437
00:51:27,000 --> 00:51:29,795
Bio-digital jazz, man.

438
00:51:30,587 --> 00:51:34,132
The IS Os, they were going 
to be my gift to the world.

439
00:51:34,216 --> 00:51:36,551
So what happened?

440
00:51:36,676 --> 00:51:40,096
Clu. Clu happened.

441
00:51:43,308 --> 00:51:46,061
Your transport to the portal 
is waiting for you.

442
00:51:46,186 --> 00:51:48,313
I don't like it when you cut it this close.

443
00:51:48,396 --> 00:51:52,442
Will you stop worrying, Tron? 
Everything's just fine.

444
00:51:52,567 --> 00:51:54,694
- Everything's under control. 
- Flynn!

445
00:51:59,491 --> 00:52:02,035
Am I still to create the perfect system?

446
00:52:03,620 --> 00:52:05,205
Yeah.

447
00:52:08,583 --> 00:52:09,709
It was a coup.

448
00:52:12,170 --> 00:52:13,171
Go.

449
00:52:27,769 --> 00:52:29,271
You've been corrupted.

450
00:52:39,364 --> 00:52:42,200
Why? Why?

451
00:52:44,953 --> 00:52:46,037
Flynn, go!

452
00:52:53,336 --> 00:52:56,298
Tron, he fought for me.

453
00:52:57,757 --> 00:53:00,010
I never saw him again.

454
00:53:00,844 --> 00:53:02,512
So why didn't you fight?

455
00:53:03,013 --> 00:53:05,015
- Hmm. 
- He did.

456
00:53:05,140 --> 00:53:06,892
Clu fed on my resistance.

457
00:53:06,975 --> 00:53:09,686
The more I fought, 
the more powerful he became.

458
00:53:10,270 --> 00:53:12,480
It was impressive, really.

459
00:53:13,106 --> 00:53:17,986
And my miracle... 
Clu saw the IS Os as an imperfection.

460
00:53:18,612 --> 00:53:20,530
So he destroyed them.

461
00:53:23,783 --> 00:53:25,452
The Purge.

462
00:53:25,994 --> 00:53:29,956
- He killed them all? 
- It was genocide.

463
00:53:30,582 --> 00:53:34,169
I tried to get back 
but I couldn't get to the portal.

464
00:53:34,753 --> 00:53:39,174
It uses massive power 
and it can't stay open forever.

465
00:53:40,008 --> 00:53:42,302
And like a safe, it...

466
00:53:42,385 --> 00:53:45,138
It can only be opened from the outside.

467
00:53:47,140 --> 00:53:49,935
It closed on me, Sam.

468
00:53:50,518 --> 00:53:52,896
That's why I never came home.

469
00:53:59,903 --> 00:54:02,072
So the portal, it activated

470
00:54:02,197 --> 00:54:04,950
when I came in. So it's open now.

471
00:54:05,033 --> 00:54:06,034
Not for long.

472
00:54:06,159 --> 00:54:08,745
Only one millicycle, about eight hours.

473
00:54:08,870 --> 00:54:12,207
So we go now. Go home. 
We make a run for it.

474
00:54:12,332 --> 00:54:14,459
- We get you out of here. 
- Don't rush.

475
00:54:14,542 --> 00:54:16,711
What do you mean? 
The portal's gonna close.

476
00:54:18,922 --> 00:54:20,507
What? What is it?

477
00:54:20,632 --> 00:54:24,678
The moment Flynn is on the Grid, Clu 
will stop at nothing to obtain his disk.

478
00:54:24,803 --> 00:54:26,846
My disk is everything, Sam.

479
00:54:26,972 --> 00:54:30,392
It's the master key. The golden ticket. 
The way out.

480
00:54:31,559 --> 00:54:35,021
- And not just for me. 
- What do you mean?

481
00:54:35,146 --> 00:54:37,857
Our worlds are more connected 
than anyone knows.

482
00:54:37,941 --> 00:54:40,151
Clu figures if I can be in...

483
00:54:40,235 --> 00:54:41,903
He can be out?

484
00:54:42,028 --> 00:54:45,573
- With my disk, it's possible. 
- And then what?

485
00:54:46,241 --> 00:54:47,951
Game over.

486
00:54:48,785 --> 00:54:51,579
The guy doesn't dig imperfection.

487
00:54:53,123 --> 00:54:55,583
What's more imperfect than our world?

488
00:54:57,168 --> 00:55:00,463
I can't let that happen. I won't.

489
00:55:00,922 --> 00:55:04,092
So what do we do? Nothing?

490
00:55:04,217 --> 00:55:07,137
It's amazing how productive 
doing nothing can be.

491
00:55:07,262 --> 00:55:10,849
Clu's planning something. 
We've known that for a while.

492
00:55:11,266 --> 00:55:12,600
Programs have been disappearing.

493
00:55:12,726 --> 00:55:15,353
There's unrest out there, 
even revolution.

494
00:55:15,437 --> 00:55:19,649
If we sit tight, Clu might 
be brought down from the inside.

495
00:55:19,774 --> 00:55:23,069
If the portal is closing, we can't 
sit around. We have to move now.

496
00:55:23,153 --> 00:55:27,949
Tell me, what brought you here, 
to the Grid?

497
00:55:28,491 --> 00:55:30,368
Alan got your page.

498
00:55:30,452 --> 00:55:33,204
I didn't send any page.

499
00:55:33,705 --> 00:55:34,914
It was Clu.

500
00:55:35,040 --> 00:55:37,917
Clu sent that page. 
That's why you're here.

501
00:55:38,001 --> 00:55:40,962
This is all his design. 
He wanted a new piece

502
00:55:41,087 --> 00:55:43,506
on the board to change the game!

503
00:55:43,631 --> 00:55:45,675
With you, he got more 
than he ever dreamed.

504
00:55:45,800 --> 00:55:50,930
This is precisely what he wants. 
Us together heading for the portal.

505
00:55:51,014 --> 00:55:55,143
It's his game now. 
The only way to win is not to play.

506
00:55:57,187 --> 00:55:59,105
Well, that's a hell of a way to live.

507
00:55:59,189 --> 00:56:00,774
But it is a way.

508
00:56:05,570 --> 00:56:07,405
We can go home.

509
00:56:09,324 --> 00:56:11,242
Don't you want that?

510
00:56:11,910 --> 00:56:17,415
Sometimes life has a way of moving you 
past things like wants and hopes.

511
00:56:18,166 --> 00:56:22,837
That's great, Dad. 
Keep telling yourself that.

512
00:56:28,510 --> 00:56:29,677
Good night, Sam.

513
00:56:52,951 --> 00:56:55,245
How can he be so afraid 
of his own creation?

514
00:56:55,370 --> 00:56:58,498
I mean, he built Clu. 
Why doesn't he just end him?

515
00:56:59,833 --> 00:57:01,876
He could, 
but it would require reintegration.

516
00:57:02,001 --> 00:57:03,461
Yeah, all right.

517
00:57:03,545 --> 00:57:07,215
Flynn would never survive the event. 
It would mean the end of them both.

518
00:57:07,674 --> 00:57:10,969
If he refuses to save himself, 
then I will.

519
00:57:15,181 --> 00:57:16,850
How?

520
00:57:16,933 --> 00:57:19,394
I'm going through the portal.

521
00:57:19,519 --> 00:57:21,229
Clu wants Flynn's disk, not mine.

522
00:57:21,354 --> 00:57:25,942
I'm going to find Alan and we'll figure 
this thing out from the other side.

523
00:57:26,067 --> 00:57:29,487
This may be Clu's game here, but 
in my world, he's gone in one keystroke.

524
00:57:29,571 --> 00:57:32,866
But I can't do anything 
unless I get to the portal.

525
00:57:34,242 --> 00:57:35,326
And my guts tell me

526
00:57:35,410 --> 00:57:39,456
that you don't want to be stuck 
in this place for eternity either.

527
00:57:41,416 --> 00:57:43,293
There's someone I once knew,

528
00:57:43,418 --> 00:57:46,504
a program named Zuse 
who fought alongside the ISOs.

529
00:57:46,629 --> 00:57:49,883
They say he can get anyone anywhere.

530
00:57:51,634 --> 00:57:53,595
How do I find him?

531
00:57:56,556 --> 00:57:58,391
This is his sector.

532
00:58:01,728 --> 00:58:04,731
Make it there alive, and he'll find you.

533
00:58:45,480 --> 00:58:47,065
Sam?

534
00:58:50,693 --> 00:58:51,945
Sam?

535
00:59:24,102 --> 00:59:28,022
All programs are required 
to present identity disks

536
00:59:28,147 --> 00:59:29,566
at city checkpoints.

537
00:59:33,903 --> 00:59:36,447
Hey, man. It's your lucky day.

538
00:59:43,413 --> 00:59:46,499
- Any program without their disk... 
- Stop him!

539
00:59:46,582 --> 00:59:48,960
...will be taken into custody.

540
00:59:50,670 --> 00:59:55,591
Any program in violation of their 
function is subject to termination.

541
00:59:56,134 --> 00:59:59,595
Sam Flynn? 
You remember me?

542
00:59:59,929 --> 01:00:03,891
- Yeah. You gave me some advice. 
- And you followed it.

543
01:00:05,351 --> 01:00:07,979
It's unfortunate we met the way we did.

544
01:00:09,314 --> 01:00:10,648
You have a good night.

545
01:00:10,773 --> 01:00:13,234
You're looking for someone.

546
01:00:15,903 --> 01:00:17,864
What makes you say that?

547
01:00:18,781 --> 01:00:20,742
Intuition.

548
01:00:22,785 --> 01:00:26,998
Sir, the sentries downtown 
have recovered Flynn's light cycle.

549
01:00:28,333 --> 01:00:31,627
We've traced it to its point of origin.

550
01:00:56,611 --> 01:00:59,489
Prepare the light runner, 
Quorra.

551
01:01:00,198 --> 01:01:02,742
We're going downtown.

552
01:01:25,139 --> 01:01:26,849
Thank you for bringing my son to me.

553
01:01:26,974 --> 01:01:28,184
Flynn!

554
01:01:28,309 --> 01:01:31,229
You don't need to go. 
I've sent him to someone we can trust.

555
01:01:31,354 --> 01:01:33,439
Quorra, there is no choice.

556
01:01:34,357 --> 01:01:36,484
I won't lose him again.

557
01:01:38,528 --> 01:01:41,697
Chaos. Good news.

558
01:02:22,613 --> 01:02:26,284
His name is Castor. If you want to speak 
to Zuse, you need to go through him.

559
01:02:26,409 --> 01:02:29,036
Where's your sense of humor, 
my friend?

560
01:02:29,120 --> 01:02:32,415
Programs are disappearing. 
Soon none of us will be left.

561
01:02:32,540 --> 01:02:35,251
Zuse can unite the factions, 
foment revolution.

562
01:02:35,334 --> 01:02:38,796
- Of course Zuse can do these things. 
- Grant me an audience.

563
01:02:38,880 --> 01:02:42,091
Your enthusiasm is intoxicating, 
my dear Bartik,

564
01:02:42,175 --> 01:02:44,886
but Zuse's time is precious.

565
01:02:46,888 --> 01:02:48,973
I brought your boy Flynn.

566
01:02:49,098 --> 01:02:54,103
If you'll excuse me for a moment, 
I have to attend to something.

567
01:02:54,228 --> 01:02:57,440
But have a drink, 
courtesy of the End of Line Club!

568
01:02:58,191 --> 01:02:59,192
It is happening.

569
01:03:00,610 --> 01:03:03,029
I'm looking for Zuse.

570
01:03:03,112 --> 01:03:05,990
Indeed. Many are.

571
01:03:06,115 --> 01:03:08,034
Where can I find him?

572
01:03:08,117 --> 01:03:12,079
This, pretty miss, is a conversation 
best had behind closed doors.

573
01:03:12,205 --> 01:03:15,583
Perhaps we should retire 
to my private lounge.

574
01:03:47,990 --> 01:03:49,659
Cozy.

575
01:04:52,638 --> 01:04:55,391
- You are Clu. 
- I am Clu.

576
01:04:55,516 --> 01:05:00,271
- You will create the perfect system. 
- I will create the perfect system.

577
01:05:03,816 --> 01:05:06,861
Together we're gonna 
change the world, man.

578
01:05:17,121 --> 01:05:18,664
Zuse has been around

579
01:05:18,789 --> 01:05:20,708
since the earliest days 
of the gaming grid.

580
01:05:21,167 --> 01:05:25,296
By necessity, 
he has to mind all the percentages.

581
01:05:25,379 --> 01:05:27,298
All the angles.

582
01:05:27,381 --> 01:05:29,216
So when do I meet him?

583
01:05:35,097 --> 01:05:38,976
After the Purge, 
I needed to reinvent myself.

584
01:05:39,060 --> 01:05:41,270
Self-preservation, you understand.

585
01:05:46,359 --> 01:05:50,947
Now, what can I do for you?

586
01:05:53,699 --> 01:05:55,701
I need to get to the portal.

587
01:05:55,826 --> 01:05:57,286
Well, it's closing quickly,

588
01:05:57,370 --> 01:05:59,997
as I'm sure you're aware.

589
01:06:00,122 --> 01:06:01,582
Tick-tock, tick-tock.

590
01:06:01,707 --> 01:06:03,959
And it's quite the journey.

591
01:06:04,043 --> 01:06:06,796
Beyond the far reaches of the Outlands.

592
01:06:06,921 --> 01:06:11,384
Your father didn't want any programs 
slipping out accidentally, did he?

593
01:06:22,603 --> 01:06:25,189
This is going to be quite the ride.

594
01:06:38,452 --> 01:06:39,704
Resist!

595
01:07:07,106 --> 01:07:08,482
Yeah!

596
01:07:48,189 --> 01:07:49,774
Go, go!

597
01:07:55,196 --> 01:07:57,281
Let's split, man.

598
01:08:17,551 --> 01:08:18,511
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

599
01:08:42,326 --> 01:08:43,577
Your disk.

600
01:08:44,620 --> 01:08:45,663
Dad, it's gone.

601
01:08:46,789 --> 01:08:47,957
It is.

602
01:08:50,918 --> 01:08:52,545
I'm sorry. I know I messed up.

603
01:08:54,755 --> 01:08:55,714
We can go back.

604
01:08:55,840 --> 01:08:58,384
- I can go back. 
- No, we stay together.

605
01:09:03,305 --> 01:09:05,307
Sam, you're really...

606
01:09:08,060 --> 01:09:10,271
You're messing with my Zen thing, man.

607
01:09:12,898 --> 01:09:13,983
She's stable.

608
01:09:20,531 --> 01:09:23,367
You ever jump a freight train?

609
01:09:24,577 --> 01:09:25,578
Huh?

610
01:09:25,661 --> 01:09:28,831
We do it your way. 
Full-on sprint to the portal.

611
01:09:28,956 --> 01:09:30,791
If we beat Clu there, we have a chance.

612
01:09:30,916 --> 01:09:32,209
Let's get her out of here.

613
01:09:51,187 --> 01:09:52,897
This'll take us there.

614
01:10:19,840 --> 01:10:22,301
- Is she gonna make it? 
- I don't know.

615
01:10:22,384 --> 01:10:25,012
I've got to identify the damaged code.

616
01:10:25,137 --> 01:10:28,682
The sequencing 
is just enormously complex.

617
01:10:29,808 --> 01:10:34,480
- But didn't you write it? 
- Some of it. The rest of it is just

618
01:10:37,358 --> 01:10:39,860
beyond me.

619
01:10:41,153 --> 01:10:43,489
She's an ISO.

620
01:10:43,572 --> 01:10:45,991
Yeah. The last ISO.

621
01:10:48,327 --> 01:10:49,703
All this time,

622
01:10:49,787 --> 01:10:52,122
you were just protecting her.

623
01:10:52,248 --> 01:10:56,210
She's the miracle, man. 
Everything I ever worked for.

624
01:10:57,628 --> 01:11:01,882
"A digital frontier 
to reshape the human condition."

625
01:11:01,966 --> 01:11:05,177
I always thought 
that was just a plug line.

626
01:11:05,260 --> 01:11:09,473
In our world, 
she could change everything.

627
01:11:10,933 --> 01:11:13,018
Yeah.

628
01:11:14,812 --> 01:11:17,231
Check that out.

629
01:11:25,406 --> 01:11:27,491
She risked herself for me.

630
01:11:29,451 --> 01:11:31,745
Some things are worth the risk.

631
01:11:45,384 --> 01:11:47,303
Look at this.

632
01:11:50,180 --> 01:11:53,767
Now, that is impressive 
if I do say so myself. Huh?

633
01:11:55,227 --> 01:11:58,397
Come on, it's gonna take a while 
for her system to reboot.

634
01:11:58,480 --> 01:12:01,650
Now it's time for you to tell me a story.

635
01:12:21,837 --> 01:12:23,964
Mom and Dad are...

636
01:12:25,716 --> 01:12:27,926
- I assume that... 
- Yeah.

637
01:12:29,136 --> 01:12:30,137
Oh.

638
01:12:31,221 --> 01:12:33,557
Mac when I was 12

639
01:12:33,682 --> 01:12:36,560
and Gram five years later.

640
01:12:39,355 --> 01:12:43,192
You remember that night 
when you didn't come home?

641
01:12:43,317 --> 01:12:44,485
- Well... 
- You said...

642
01:12:44,610 --> 01:12:46,653
I said I'd show you the Grid.

643
01:12:48,739 --> 01:12:51,033
You should have seen this place 
back then.

644
01:12:52,034 --> 01:12:57,331
I couldn't wait to show it to you. 
Could not wait.

645
01:12:57,414 --> 01:12:59,917
Must have been something 
before Clu screwed it up.

646
01:13:00,042 --> 01:13:01,710
No, no, he...

647
01:13:03,545 --> 01:13:06,173
He's me. I screwed it up.

648
01:13:07,716 --> 01:13:09,802
Chasing after perfection.

649
01:13:11,887 --> 01:13:15,557
Chasing after 
what was right in front of me.

650
01:13:16,183 --> 01:13:18,060
Right in front of me.

651
01:13:19,978 --> 01:13:22,314
Look what you've accomplished.

652
01:13:22,731 --> 01:13:24,483
It's incredible.

653
01:13:24,566 --> 01:13:25,943
Sam,

654
01:13:26,902 --> 01:13:30,197
I'd have given it all up 
for one more day with you.

655
01:13:43,335 --> 01:13:44,336
Hey.

656
01:13:44,962 --> 01:13:47,131
Remember your old Ducati?

657
01:13:47,339 --> 01:13:50,509
You kidding? Not a day goes by 
I don't think about that bike.

658
01:13:50,634 --> 01:13:54,596
- Yeah, well, I'm fixing her up. 
- You busted my bike?

659
01:13:54,680 --> 01:14:00,352
Twenty years in the shed, no tarp. 
I mean, she needed a little love.

660
01:14:01,979 --> 01:14:03,522
How does she run?

661
01:14:03,605 --> 01:14:07,568
Well, when I'm done, better than ever.

662
01:14:08,861 --> 01:14:11,363
Well, man, I'd like to see that.

663
01:14:13,031 --> 01:14:14,533
You will.

664
01:14:17,953 --> 01:14:18,954
Here.

665
01:14:19,288 --> 01:14:21,748
She's rebooting. Give her this.

666
01:14:21,832 --> 01:14:25,627
The old man's gonna knock on the sky, 
listen to the sound.

667
01:14:52,529 --> 01:14:55,532
I realize that our alliance is,

668
01:14:55,657 --> 01:14:57,075
at times,

669
01:14:57,159 --> 01:14:58,619
uneasy.

670
01:14:58,744 --> 01:15:00,704
But always necessary.

671
01:15:02,498 --> 01:15:04,124
You know you need me

672
01:15:04,249 --> 01:15:06,251
right where I am.

673
01:15:06,335 --> 01:15:07,753
Clu?

674
01:15:08,378 --> 01:15:10,547
Of course you're right.

675
01:15:11,340 --> 01:15:13,091
Enjoy the drink.

676
01:15:20,766 --> 01:15:22,893
End of line, man.

677
01:15:32,527 --> 01:15:35,572
Hey. It's okay. We're safe for now.

678
01:15:35,697 --> 01:15:38,283
We're heading east towards the portal.

679
01:15:41,036 --> 01:15:42,913
Clu has the disk.

680
01:15:43,580 --> 01:15:46,416
Once I get out, I can shut him down.

681
01:15:48,585 --> 01:15:52,089
I should never have sent you to Zuse. 
It was a mistake.

682
01:15:52,214 --> 01:15:54,675
It's okay. I've made a few myself.

683
01:16:00,180 --> 01:16:01,765
Where is he?

684
01:16:02,766 --> 01:16:05,602
I think he's knocking on the sky.

685
01:16:05,769 --> 01:16:07,854
And listening to the sound.

686
01:16:09,147 --> 01:16:10,482
Yeah.

687
01:16:11,149 --> 01:16:13,110
How did you find him?

688
01:16:22,619 --> 01:16:23,704
It's okay.

689
01:16:26,498 --> 01:16:27,916
I know.

690
01:16:33,171 --> 01:16:35,465
It was during the Purge.

691
01:16:36,675 --> 01:16:38,593
Clu was relentless.

692
01:16:39,011 --> 01:16:42,306
The Black Guard were executing ISOs 
in the streets.

693
01:16:43,515 --> 01:16:46,226
Everyone I knew disappeared.

694
01:16:48,895 --> 01:16:50,939
Then they came for me.

695
01:16:53,692 --> 01:16:55,611
So I ran.

696
01:16:56,695 --> 01:16:59,990
A sympathetic program 
smuggled me out of the city.

697
01:17:00,073 --> 01:17:02,576
But soon they had me surrounded.

698
01:17:02,701 --> 01:17:04,661
I prepared for the end.

699
01:17:04,870 --> 01:17:07,205
And just as everything was going dark,

700
01:17:07,873 --> 01:17:10,792
I felt a hand on my shoulder.

701
01:17:13,545 --> 01:17:15,547
And when I opened my eyes,

702
01:17:17,049 --> 01:17:21,386
standing above me was the Creator.

703
01:17:24,056 --> 01:17:25,807
Your father.

704
01:17:27,434 --> 01:17:29,061
He saved me.

705
01:17:33,357 --> 01:17:36,818
I guess you could say I'm a rescue.

706
01:17:55,921 --> 01:17:57,714
Hell of a view.

707
01:17:57,881 --> 01:18:00,676
It used to let us know 
that Flynn was here.

708
01:18:00,759 --> 01:18:03,095
It became the symbol 
of something bigger,

709
01:18:03,261 --> 01:18:06,515
something better than this world.

710
01:18:07,307 --> 01:18:09,768
I've never been this close before.

711
01:18:10,936 --> 01:18:13,480
It's how I imagine a sunrise to be.

712
01:18:13,605 --> 01:18:15,899
Ah, trust me. There's no comparison.

713
01:18:19,361 --> 01:18:21,279
What's it like?

714
01:18:23,323 --> 01:18:25,784
- The sun? 
- Yeah.

715
01:18:26,660 --> 01:18:28,620
Man...

716
01:18:30,247 --> 01:18:32,833
I've never had to describe it before.

717
01:18:34,876 --> 01:18:36,044
Warm.

718
01:18:38,130 --> 01:18:39,673
Radiant.

719
01:18:42,467 --> 01:18:44,261
Beautiful.

720
01:19:09,911 --> 01:19:12,622
Get below! Move!

721
01:19:14,499 --> 01:19:16,793
This isn't supposed to be here.

722
01:19:21,047 --> 01:19:24,384
- What happened? 
- A new course.

723
01:19:35,312 --> 01:19:36,688
What is this?

724
01:19:37,689 --> 01:19:39,316
Clu can't create programs.

725
01:19:39,649 --> 01:19:42,235
He can only destroy or repurpose them.

726
01:19:43,195 --> 01:19:45,739
Repurpose them for what?

727
01:19:49,159 --> 01:19:52,996
Battalion 6, 
report to Staging Area Alpha.

728
01:19:56,208 --> 01:19:57,709
He's building an army.

729
01:19:58,752 --> 01:20:00,045
Let's go.

730
01:20:00,170 --> 01:20:05,342
All high-level functions, 
report immediately to combat stations.

731
01:20:30,534 --> 01:20:32,786
- Goodbye. 
- Quorra!

732
01:20:33,286 --> 01:20:36,289
- What's she doing? 
- Removing herself from the equation.

733
01:20:54,432 --> 01:20:55,851
Tron.

734
01:20:57,644 --> 01:20:59,729
He's alive.

735
01:21:00,355 --> 01:21:02,816
Throne ship is approaching.

736
01:21:05,735 --> 01:21:09,406
- We can't just let her go. 
- No, Sam, there's another way.

737
01:21:10,115 --> 01:21:12,492
Throne ship is approaching.

738
01:21:23,753 --> 01:21:25,839
Well done, sir.

739
01:21:40,103 --> 01:21:42,564
I've never seen anything like it.

740
01:21:43,064 --> 01:21:46,693
Humbly, sir, I know 
you have greater designs

741
01:21:46,818 --> 01:21:49,362
than any of us understand.

742
01:21:50,572 --> 01:21:51,990
What does it do?

743
01:22:17,891 --> 01:22:19,768
She's gonna wind up like one of them.

744
01:22:20,310 --> 01:22:21,478
Keep moving, Sam.

745
01:22:32,155 --> 01:22:35,950
You are a very rare bird, 
aren't you, now?

746
01:22:44,209 --> 01:22:45,919
Where's your disk?

747
01:22:48,546 --> 01:22:50,340
Where is he?

748
01:22:52,258 --> 01:22:54,969
It must have been so lonely out there.

749
01:22:56,262 --> 01:22:58,848
How tragic to be the only one.

750
01:23:00,767 --> 01:23:03,645
I've seen what users 
are capable of, Clu.

751
01:23:04,479 --> 01:23:06,690
You don't belong with them.

752
01:23:08,441 --> 01:23:13,238
You'll have to excuse me. You arrived 
just as I was preparing a little toast.

753
01:23:28,294 --> 01:23:30,255
Greetings, programs!

754
01:23:37,971 --> 01:23:39,472
Your disk.

755
01:23:49,190 --> 01:23:50,608
He's taking all of this with him.

756
01:23:51,192 --> 01:23:53,027
He's figured out how to do it.

757
01:23:53,153 --> 01:23:55,030
And whatever we find there,

758
01:23:55,196 --> 01:23:57,323
there our system will grow!

759
01:23:57,407 --> 01:24:00,994
There our system will blossom!

760
01:24:02,245 --> 01:24:03,747
Do this.

761
01:24:03,872 --> 01:24:06,499
Prove yourselves. 
Prove yourselves to me!

762
01:24:06,583 --> 01:24:08,168
Be loyal to me!

763
01:24:08,710 --> 01:24:11,504
And I will never betray you!

764
01:24:24,100 --> 01:24:25,685
Dad, we have to get your disk.

765
01:24:25,894 --> 01:24:28,605
We gotta get to the portal. 
You shut them down outside.

766
01:24:28,730 --> 01:24:31,357
Even if I make it, you won't 
last in here, nor will Quorra.

767
01:24:31,483 --> 01:24:34,694
- We'll be all right. Come on. 
- I'm not going home without you.

768
01:24:35,862 --> 01:24:37,113
Sam...

769
01:24:37,697 --> 01:24:39,449
The same team.

770
01:24:40,408 --> 01:24:41,743
Remember?

771
01:24:43,912 --> 01:24:45,914
I was afraid you were gonna say that.

772
01:24:46,998 --> 01:24:49,084
Meet me on the flight deck in five 
and get us some wheels.

773
01:24:49,209 --> 01:24:51,377
Wheels? What's your plan?

774
01:24:52,253 --> 01:24:54,631
I'm a user. I'll improvise.

775
01:24:56,257 --> 01:24:58,176
Maximize efficiency.

776
01:24:59,594 --> 01:25:02,472
Rid the new system of its imperfection!

777
01:25:05,099 --> 01:25:08,603
My vision is clear, fellow programs.

778
01:25:09,604 --> 01:25:13,733
Out there is a new world!

779
01:25:14,484 --> 01:25:17,153
Out there is our victory!

780
01:25:18,613 --> 01:25:20,114
Out there

781
01:25:22,242 --> 01:25:24,702
is our destiny.

782
01:26:08,371 --> 01:26:11,040
Identify yourself, program.

783
01:26:14,252 --> 01:26:17,297
I'm not a program. 
My name is Sam Flynn.

784
01:26:41,321 --> 01:26:43,740
- Identify yourself. 
- I'm taking this vehicle.

785
01:26:43,865 --> 01:26:45,742
You are not authorized.

786
01:26:48,077 --> 01:26:50,496
Right away, sir.

787
01:26:51,497 --> 01:26:54,834
Watch your step, please, 
as you board the aircraft.

788
01:27:06,137 --> 01:27:08,348
Long live the users.

789
01:27:15,146 --> 01:27:17,941
Master key disengaged.

790
01:27:18,232 --> 01:27:20,568
Master key disengaged.

791
01:27:20,860 --> 01:27:24,948
I came with a girl, a program. 
Where is she?

792
01:27:27,492 --> 01:27:29,160
Sam! Go!

793
01:27:55,436 --> 01:27:57,730
- Why are you here? 
- We gotta get to the flight deck.

794
01:27:57,855 --> 01:27:59,107
Clu will be here any minute.

795
01:27:59,232 --> 01:28:01,067
- We'll never make it. 
- Come on.

796
01:28:07,448 --> 01:28:08,783
Come on, kiddo.

797
01:28:12,453 --> 01:28:15,123
Radical, man.

798
01:28:22,964 --> 01:28:24,424
Hurry!

799
01:28:24,507 --> 01:28:26,175
Made it.

800
01:28:44,986 --> 01:28:47,989
Try and hold on to this. 
You're gonna need it.

801
01:28:53,036 --> 01:28:56,664
You got this, Quorra. It's all in the wrist.

802
01:29:11,596 --> 01:29:15,141
Light fighter 5. 
Unauthorized launch.

803
01:29:36,204 --> 01:29:38,247
Death to the user!

804
01:30:02,688 --> 01:30:03,898
Head towards the light, Quorra.

805
01:30:06,818 --> 01:30:08,611
Where did you learn that move?

806
01:30:08,736 --> 01:30:11,364
ENCOM Tower. Couple of nights ago.

807
01:30:13,866 --> 01:30:16,494
ENCOM Tower. Huh.

808
01:30:18,287 --> 01:30:19,497
Light jets.

809
01:30:20,081 --> 01:30:21,457
Here they come.

810
01:30:22,416 --> 01:30:24,794
- Sam, take the turret. 
- Huh?

811
01:30:33,052 --> 01:30:35,346
Man, I'm all over this.

812
01:30:38,307 --> 01:30:39,433
Yeah!

813
01:30:49,360 --> 01:30:52,613
Yeah! One down, five to go!

814
01:30:58,369 --> 01:30:59,370
Nice.

815
01:31:08,838 --> 01:31:10,006
We gotta split them up!

816
01:31:10,006 --> 01:31:10,756
We gotta split them up!

817
01:31:30,526 --> 01:31:31,903
Come on.

818
01:31:36,115 --> 01:31:38,159
Have a nice swim!

819
01:31:44,332 --> 01:31:46,334
We gotta get behind them!

820
01:31:47,001 --> 01:31:48,794
Whoo!

821
01:31:54,175 --> 01:31:57,345
- I don't think this is a good idea. 
- You're probably right.

822
01:32:03,184 --> 01:32:04,602
He's gaining on us!

823
01:32:35,424 --> 01:32:36,801
Yes!

824
01:32:48,354 --> 01:32:49,355
It's jammed.

825
01:32:54,986 --> 01:32:58,239
Tron. What have you become?

826
01:33:03,286 --> 01:33:04,579
Oh, come on.

827
01:33:07,790 --> 01:33:10,334
Flynn, go!

828
01:33:13,796 --> 01:33:16,007
Rinzler! Take the shot!

829
01:33:18,926 --> 01:33:20,595
Finish the game!

830
01:33:28,477 --> 01:33:30,438
Roll!

831
01:33:42,825 --> 01:33:45,536
I fight for the users.

832
01:33:55,504 --> 01:33:57,965
That's it. It's over.

833
01:33:58,966 --> 01:33:59,967
It's over!

834
01:34:02,136 --> 01:34:03,929
Whoo!

835
01:34:11,437 --> 01:34:14,565
Quorra, there's something 
I need you to do.

836
01:34:54,772 --> 01:34:56,691
Let's get you home, Dad.

837
01:35:00,736 --> 01:35:02,029
There it is.

838
01:35:02,696 --> 01:35:06,909
- Take her down. 
- Hang on. This could be rough.

839
01:35:59,086 --> 01:36:01,297
This is mine.

840
01:36:06,510 --> 01:36:08,596
I had a feeling you'd be here!

841
01:36:08,763 --> 01:36:12,558
The cycles 
haven't been kind, have they?

842
01:36:13,476 --> 01:36:15,728
Oh, you don't look so bad.

843
01:36:17,062 --> 01:36:20,608
I did everything. 
Everything you ever asked.

844
01:36:21,233 --> 01:36:22,943
I know you did.

845
01:36:23,068 --> 01:36:26,363
- I executed the plan! 
- As you saw it.

846
01:36:26,489 --> 01:36:31,660
You... You promised that we 
would change the world together.

847
01:36:32,077 --> 01:36:34,205
- You broke your promise. 
- I know.

848
01:36:34,455 --> 01:36:35,831
I understand that now.

849
01:36:35,956 --> 01:36:39,376
I took this system 
to its maximum potential.

850
01:36:39,460 --> 01:36:41,587
I created the perfect system!

851
01:36:41,712 --> 01:36:45,424
The thing about perfection 
is that it's unknowable.

852
01:36:46,634 --> 01:36:51,222
It's impossible but it's also 
right in front of us all the time.

853
01:36:51,347 --> 01:36:54,975
You wouldn't know that 
because I didn't when I created you.

854
01:36:56,811 --> 01:36:58,521
I'm sorry, Clu.

855
01:37:00,648 --> 01:37:03,067
I'm sorry.

856
01:37:24,797 --> 01:37:25,965
Go.

857
01:37:39,436 --> 01:37:40,521
Clu!

858
01:37:41,522 --> 01:37:43,774
Remember what you came for.

859
01:37:57,788 --> 01:37:58,789
Dad!

860
01:37:58,914 --> 01:38:00,374
You knew I'd beat you.

861
01:38:00,541 --> 01:38:03,586
And still you did all this? For him.

862
01:38:09,758 --> 01:38:11,010
No.

863
01:38:16,599 --> 01:38:17,850
No!

864
01:38:17,975 --> 01:38:19,310
Why?

865
01:38:21,020 --> 01:38:22,354
He's my son.

866
01:38:28,736 --> 01:38:29,737
Go!

867
01:38:35,618 --> 01:38:36,702
Dad!

868
01:38:43,250 --> 01:38:45,377
Sam! It's time!

869
01:38:45,669 --> 01:38:46,795
No!

870
01:38:47,212 --> 01:38:49,923
Sam, it's what he wants.

871
01:38:52,009 --> 01:38:53,927
I'm not leaving you!

872
01:38:54,345 --> 01:38:55,804
Take her!

873
01:39:07,274 --> 01:39:08,859
Yes!

874
01:39:36,553 --> 01:39:38,222
Goodbye, kiddo.

875
01:40:09,545 --> 01:40:11,422
No!

876
01:41:22,367 --> 01:41:24,161
Al?

877
01:41:28,582 --> 01:41:30,375
You paged me?

878
01:41:30,918 --> 01:41:32,544
Yeah.

879
01:41:33,378 --> 01:41:35,631
I need you at ENCOM at 8:00 a.m.

880
01:41:37,591 --> 01:41:39,093
What about the board?

881
01:41:40,761 --> 01:41:42,679
You're chairman now.

882
01:41:45,015 --> 01:41:47,434
I'm taking the company back, Alan.

883
01:41:51,563 --> 01:41:52,731
Oh, and...

884
01:41:54,066 --> 01:41:55,692
You were right.

885
01:41:57,402 --> 01:41:58,779
About what?

886
01:42:00,447 --> 01:42:02,032
About everything.

887
01:42:49,580 --> 01:42:51,748
Software giant ENCOM International

888
01:42:51,832 --> 01:42:53,125
has reinstated 
Alan Bradley as chairman.

889
01:42:53,208 --> 01:42:54,710
The justice department 
announced today

890
01:42:54,793 --> 01:42:56,587
that it'll begin 
a full-on investigation

891
01:42:56,670 --> 01:42:59,464
into the underground movement 
known as "Flynn Lives"

892
01:42:59,548 --> 01:43:02,426
Lead by a mysterious 
online figure known as ZackAttack

893
01:43:02,509 --> 01:43:04,803
this renegade group 
has been held responsible

894
01:43:04,887 --> 01:43:07,306
for acts of subversion 
against ENCOM

895
01:43:07,514 --> 01:43:09,474
Flynn lives! Flynn lives!

896
01:43:09,808 --> 01:43:12,769
Greetings. My name is ZackAttack.

897
01:43:13,145 --> 01:43:16,023
Previously I have been communicating 
to you through message in code.

898
01:43:16,648 --> 01:43:19,067
But as of this morning 
the landscape has become

899
01:43:19,151 --> 01:43:21,737
too difficult and dangerous to operate in.

900
01:43:23,030 --> 01:43:24,323
I want to thank the millions of you

901
01:43:24,406 --> 01:43:27,201
who have helped to keep 
Kevin Flynn's spirit alive,

902
01:43:27,951 --> 01:43:32,039
And especially those of you 
who've been with us from the beginning.

903
01:44:10,786 --> 01:44:13,789
The "Flynn Lives" movement 
was born out of loss.

904
01:44:14,331 --> 01:44:17,250
But Kevin's genius inspired us all.

905
01:44:17,334 --> 01:44:20,671
He saw worlds beyond 
what we believed to be possible.

906
01:44:21,421 --> 01:44:25,050
When he disappeared 
that future was taken from all of us.

907
01:44:26,301 --> 01:44:28,553
Alan Bradley tried to continue 
Flynn's legacy.

908
01:44:29,346 --> 01:44:33,392
But when ENCOM started losing 
the software game to Dillinger Systems

909
01:44:34,226 --> 01:44:36,770
the shareholders demanded a change.

910
01:44:59,209 --> 01:45:00,919
ENCOM was founded 
around a chess game.

911
01:45:01,420 --> 01:45:04,214
Your plan represents 
everything Flynn was against.

912
01:45:04,339 --> 01:45:06,341
That spirit totally got away from the top brass.

913
01:45:06,883 --> 01:45:08,969
So we're all writing Flynn off.

914
01:45:09,678 --> 01:45:11,638
You'll have my resignation 
by the end of the day.

915
01:45:11,722 --> 01:45:13,640
How many employees 
are you letting go?

916
01:45:15,225 --> 01:45:17,602
With Flynn gone we were no longer 
in the video game business.

917
01:45:17,936 --> 01:45:19,396
It was all corporate.

918
01:45:19,479 --> 01:45:22,149
I knew it was over 
when they brought the cubicles back.

919
01:45:23,608 --> 01:45:26,570
I guess getting fired is the 
best thing that ever happened to me.

920
01:45:26,653 --> 01:45:29,448
But you were one of the first 
of ENCOM's employees ...

921
01:45:34,036 --> 01:45:36,038
Sure. I have this nickname.

922
01:45:36,121 --> 01:45:37,205
What do you want to start from? 
Just that?

923
01:45:37,289 --> 01:45:38,331
- That question?

924
01:45:39,624 --> 01:45:42,085
Anyway, we were talking about what? 
Oh, my TRON nickname.

925
01:45:42,169 --> 01:45:43,378
Right.

926
01:45:44,463 --> 01:45:49,259
I created a self monitoring 
computer security system back in '82.

927
01:45:49,343 --> 01:45:50,719
I called it TRON.

928
01:45:50,844 --> 01:45:52,012
Well the weird thing is ...

929
01:45:52,596 --> 01:45:54,181
Flynn took to calling me TRON.

930
01:45:54,431 --> 01:45:57,976
All the programmers ran with it 
and the nickname stayed with me for years.

931
01:45:59,603 --> 01:46:00,812
Speaking of which,

932
01:46:00,896 --> 01:46:03,607
it's been 7 years since 
you've last seen Kevin Flynn.

933
01:46:04,232 --> 01:46:07,861
Do you think there's anyone out there 
who still thinks he's alive?

934
01:46:19,373 --> 01:46:20,457
Mom, it's doing it again.

935
01:46:21,708 --> 01:46:25,921
You created the "Flynn Lives" virus 
that attacked 40 million PCs last year.

936
01:46:26,630 --> 01:46:29,466
I like to think of it as more of 
an easter egg than a virus.

937
01:46:30,092 --> 01:46:31,760
Was the group "Flynn Lives" involved?

938
01:46:31,843 --> 01:46:33,345
No. I acted alone.

939
01:46:34,054 --> 01:46:35,222
It was so cool.

940
01:46:39,226 --> 01:46:40,394
Are you ZackAttack?

941
01:46:42,896 --> 01:46:44,147
No.

942
01:46:44,481 --> 01:46:46,983
And if I was there's no way 
you would have ever found me.

943
01:46:47,067 --> 01:46:48,151
"Isolated Thinker"?

944
01:46:48,235 --> 01:46:50,821
Well, "Isolated Thinker" helped me 
pay my legal fees and kept me out of jail.

945
01:46:51,405 --> 01:46:53,907
But you did hack into the ENCOM mainframe

946
01:46:53,990 --> 01:46:55,325
on behalf of the group, correct?

947
01:46:55,409 --> 01:46:56,827
You can't talk about that.

948
01:46:58,787 --> 01:47:02,374
How do you feel about the 
"Flynn Lives" movement now, today?

949
01:47:02,457 --> 01:47:05,001
What movement? It's totally dead.

950
01:47:08,713 --> 01:47:12,843
Publicly ENCOM and I have acknowledged 
the fact that he is gone.

951
01:47:13,677 --> 01:47:17,556
His disappearance was a massive blow, 
personally and professionally.

952
01:47:17,556 --> 01:47:20,058
This was a man with an entire world

953
01:47:20,225 --> 01:47:22,060
waiting to see what he was going to do next.

954
01:47:22,144 --> 01:47:25,981
So, yeah, I think something's a little weird 
that he just totally vanishes.

955
01:47:26,064 --> 01:47:27,232
Well, I don't think he's dead.

956
01:47:27,774 --> 01:47:31,486
Is there anyone in the movement 
who has seen Kevin Flynn alive and well?

957
01:47:31,570 --> 01:47:34,739
- Where do you think he is? 
- I don't know. They know!

958
01:47:35,449 --> 01:47:38,618
I'm still trying to see what part 
they might have in this,

959
01:47:38,702 --> 01:47:40,495
but I can't really ...

960
01:47:40,787 --> 01:47:43,874
I don't know anything about the conspiracy theories 
or that sort of thing ...

961
01:47:43,957 --> 01:47:46,251
- Okay, well that's the way I want to say it, though

962
01:47:46,334 --> 01:47:48,545
I mean, I want to say ...

963
01:47:50,130 --> 01:47:51,798
Flynn possessed

964
01:47:53,008 --> 01:47:55,468
an undying commitment to 
his family and his friends.

965
01:47:55,552 --> 01:47:57,637
No, Flynn poss-

966
01:48:00,599 --> 01:48:02,476
I need to start again ...

967
01:48:06,980 --> 01:48:09,774
Years passed, and the voices 
asking what really happened

968
01:48:09,858 --> 01:48:11,818
gradually fell silent.

969
01:48:12,527 --> 01:48:15,405
Flynn was gone. 
Not dead, but gone.

970
01:48:15,739 --> 01:48:18,116
We needed to find a new way 
to reach people.

971
01:48:18,200 --> 01:48:20,285
We needed to think like Kevin.

972
01:48:20,494 --> 01:48:22,746
Evolution is the greatest force 
in the material world.

973
01:48:23,997 --> 01:48:26,917
Evolution will provide us 
the next step of intelligent life.

974
01:48:27,292 --> 01:48:30,170
But it will come 
from somewhere unexpected.

975
01:48:33,048 --> 01:48:35,884
And then 
something wonderful happened.

976
01:48:48,855 --> 01:48:50,857
Flynn came back.

977
01:48:51,858 --> 01:48:53,902
Just a little bit younger.

978
01:49:01,326 --> 01:49:03,203
Sam Flynn inspired us.

979
01:49:03,745 --> 01:49:05,288
Every year he'd find a new way to

980
01:49:05,372 --> 01:49:08,083
infuriate ENCOM's board with his pranks.

981
01:49:11,002 --> 01:49:14,089
He started doing the work 
that we had almost given up 10 years ago.

982
01:49:18,927 --> 01:49:20,804
We began the search again.

983
01:49:31,898 --> 01:49:34,276
This time, millions of you joined us.

984
01:49:46,246 --> 01:49:48,581
Flynn lives! Flynn lives!

985
01:49:50,667 --> 01:49:54,713
Today, Sam Flynn has brought us 
a little closer to his father

986
01:49:54,796 --> 01:49:56,339
by reclaiming ENCOM.

987
01:49:58,508 --> 01:50:00,552
But these are confusing times.

988
01:50:01,720 --> 01:50:03,680
We may have not found Kevin Flynn,

989
01:50:05,432 --> 01:50:08,018
but Flynn lives in all of us 
who want to believe in him.

990
01:50:09,978 --> 01:50:12,397
This will be my last transmission.

991
01:50:35,086 --> 01:50:36,212
Greetings, program.

992
01:50:38,214 --> 01:50:39,215
What are you doing?

993
01:50:39,299 --> 01:50:41,801
Wiping the hard drive, 
what does it look like I'm doing.

994
01:50:41,885 --> 01:50:44,179
Looks like you are destroying 
ENCOM corporate property

995
01:50:44,262 --> 01:50:45,305
that's what it looks like you're doing.

996
01:50:45,388 --> 01:50:47,474
After the news, 
I didn't know who to trust.

997
01:50:47,557 --> 01:50:49,100
Oh, relax.

998
01:50:49,184 --> 01:50:50,560
There's no need to panic.

999
01:50:50,643 --> 01:50:51,686
That's easy for you to say.

1000
01:50:51,770 --> 01:50:54,522
Your house isn't filled with 
20 years of corporate espionage.

1001
01:50:54,606 --> 01:50:57,692
Oh? And I'm the one that's still 
employed by ENCOM.

1002
01:50:57,776 --> 01:51:00,403
If my board finds out that I've been 
funding your little operation ...

1003
01:51:00,487 --> 01:51:03,490
Our little operation, 
Isolated Thinker.

1004
01:51:04,115 --> 01:51:05,200
Yeah.

1005
01:51:08,953 --> 01:51:10,455
So what do we do now?

1006
01:51:10,789 --> 01:51:14,876
Well, I think it's time that we put 
Flynn Lives behind us.

1007
01:51:15,669 --> 01:51:18,046
What about the page at the arcade? 
I'm not giving up now.

1008
01:51:18,129 --> 01:51:21,424
Flynn may not be back, but I am. 
Sam's made me chairman.

1009
01:51:21,508 --> 01:51:24,260
Oh yeah, I saw that on the news. 
Congratulations.

1010
01:51:24,344 --> 01:51:25,929
And I want you to join me.

1011
01:51:29,182 --> 01:51:32,102
Think of yourself as 
ENCOM's moral compass.

1012
01:51:33,311 --> 01:51:36,147
- What about Mackey? 
- He's out. They're all out.

1013
01:51:36,231 --> 01:51:37,607
We have our company back.

1014
01:51:40,110 --> 01:51:41,611
And that Dillinger kid?

1015
01:51:42,028 --> 01:51:45,240
Well, Junior earned his place. He stays.

1016
01:51:46,116 --> 01:51:47,742
What about you?

1017
01:51:51,079 --> 01:51:52,914
Sign me up, TRON.

1018
01:51:53,289 --> 01:51:54,999
Good to have you back, RAM.

1019
01:51:59,713 --> 01:52:03,216
One thing. Why do you think 
Flynn gave you the cool nickname?

1020
01:52:05,176 --> 01:52:06,886
We are reporting live from ENCOM offices,

1021
01:52:06,970 --> 01:52:10,014
where sources indicate that Sam Flynn 
is on his way here

1022
01:52:10,098 --> 01:52:11,141
to address the board.

1023
01:52:11,224 --> 01:52:13,893
This will be Sam's first public appearance since-

1024
01:52:17,230 --> 01:52:19,274
Over here, Sam, one second, over here.

1025
01:52:21,151 --> 01:52:22,235
How's it going fellas?

1026
01:52:24,279 --> 01:52:25,572
Have a good day.

1027
01:52:26,781 --> 01:52:30,201
What do you think your father 
would have said about your actions today?

1028
01:52:30,827 --> 01:52:34,289
Sam, do you have any statements 
for the press today?

